  {
    "note": 4,
    "anchor": "*Reason:* divide the accumulation balance by $q$ to expose the residence time $\\tau$."
  },
  {
    "note": 4,
    "anchor": "Multiply by integrating factor $e^{t/\\tau}$:"
  },
  {
    "note": 4,
    "anchor": "*Reason:* the product derivative collects the derivative and concentration terms."
  },
  {
    "note": 4,
    "anchor": "*Reason:* collect both removal mechanisms and divide by their combined coefficient. Thus the effective time constant is $\\tau_r=\\tau/(1+k\\tau)$ and concentration gain is $K_p=1/(1+k\\tau)$."
  },
  {
    "note": 4,
    "anchor": "**Step 4: transform the perturbation balance.**"
  },
  {
    "note": 4,
    "anchor": "*Reason:* the Laplace transform replaces $\\dot c'$ by $s\\bar c$. The pole $p$ gives the exponential decay rate."
  },
  {
    "note": 4,
    "anchor": "The inert tank has unit concentration gain and pole $-1/\\tau$. Reaction moves the pole farther left and reduces the gain. A faster response therefore does not imply more reactant survives. Taking $k\\rightarrow0$ recovers the inert-tank model."
  },
  {
    "note": 4,
    "anchor": "These conclusions require constant $q,V,k$ and negligible thermal coupling. Changing temperature or flow generally changes the coefficients; second-order kinetics requires local linearisation."
  },
